1
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:03,900
A network diagram tells you what is connected.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,320
It rarely tells you why a particular packet disappeared.

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:10,320
Faultline makes that chain of events visible.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:14,500
This is an original MoonBit engine, compiled to WebAssembly.

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,440
In this experiment, twelve packets cross a six-node network.

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:21,220
A bridge fails at fifty-two milliseconds.

7
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:22,760
Nine packets arrive.

8
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:23,780
Three do not.

9
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,960
The healthy-network comparison delivers all twelve.

10
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:28,940
Select packet three.

11
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:34,240
It enters at sixteen milliseconds, reaches North at thirty-four, and
starts crossing the bridge.

12
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,940
The link fails exactly when it would arrive.

13
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:40,580
Under this model's explicit rule, failure wins.

14
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:45,360
The packet's entire story is here, with times and a counterfactual
outcome.

15
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:47,880
Now change when the failure happens.

16
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,480
These bars are twenty-five fresh MoonBit simulations.

17
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:53,740
They are not an estimated curve.

18
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:59,760
If the bridge is already down before traffic starts, all twelve
packets arrive through the alternative route.

19
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:04,060
Earlier failure can mean less loss, because nothing is caught in
flight.

20
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:06,940
Connectivity is only part of the story.

21
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:11,420
Send all twelve packets at once into a queue with room for three.

22
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:15,400
Nine are rejected immediately, even with every link healthy.

23
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,540
The event trace distinguishes queue overflow from a broken route.

24
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:22,760
Faultline also computes minimal failure sets.

25
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:26,260
Apply this two-link cut, and no path remains.

26
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:29,980
Remove either member of the cut, and connectivity returns.

27
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,540
These are graph results, separate from the traffic simulation.

28
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:35,480
Reset to healthy conditions.

29
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,760
Export the complete experiment, including topology, parameters and
event evidence.

30
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:46,080
Importing recomputes the results in MoonBit instead of trusting a
saved answer.

31
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,160
Everything runs locally.

32
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,200
No service, account or API key is needed.

33
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,700
This is an explicit learning model, not a production-network
predictor.

34
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:02,600
The source, tests and assumptions are open, so every result can be
inspected, reproduced and challenged.
